**Defensive copy (`copyOf`)**:
```java
public final class Report {
    private final List<String> lines;

    public Report(List<String> lines) {
        this.lines = List.copyOf(lines);  // immutable snapshot
    }

    public List<String> getLines() {
        return lines;  // safe to expose
    }
}
```

**Building dynamically, freezing at the end**:
```java
List<String> temp = new ArrayList<>();
temp.add("first");
temp.add("second");
List<String> frozen = List.copyOf(temp);
```

**Converting streams** (Java 10+ has `Stream.toList()`):
```java
List<String> result = stream.collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());
// Or, Java 10+:
List<String> result = stream.toList();  // returns unmodifiable list (not the same class as List.of, but immutable)
```

Pre-Java 11, making an HTTP request in the JDK meant `HttpURLConnection`:

```java
// The old, painful way
URL url = new URL("https://api.example.com/users/42");
HttpURLConnection conn = (HttpURLConnection) url.openConnection();
conn.setRequestMethod("GET");
conn.setRequestProperty("Accept", "application/json");
conn.setConnectTimeout(5000);
conn.setReadTimeout(10000);
int status = conn.getResponseCode();

try (var is = conn.getInputStream();
     var reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(is))) {
    String body = reader.lines().collect(Collectors.joining());
    // parse JSON...
}
conn.disconnect();
```

Problems:
- No HTTP/2 support.
- No built-in async.
- Manual stream plumbing for every request.
- No connection pooling across requests.
- Brittle error handling (exceptions vs. status codes).
- No WebSocket.

Most projects reached for Apache HttpClient, OkHttp, or Spring's `RestTemplate`/`WebClient`. Java 11's `HttpClient` finally gives the JDK a competitive built-in option.

**Key design wins**:
- Builder pattern — readable, IDE-friendly.
- HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 (automatic negotiation via ALPN).
- Sync (`send`) and async (`sendAsync`) APIs.
- Immutable, thread-safe `HttpClient` — create once, reuse everywhere.
- Connection pooling and HTTP/2 multiplexing built in.
- WebSocket support.
- Body handlers for common shapes: `String`, `byte[]`, `Path` (stream to file), `InputStream`, etc.

The async API returns `CompletableFuture<HttpResponse<T>>` — compose, combine, handle errors using the full CF toolkit.

**Single async call**:
```java
client.sendAsync(req, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString())
    .thenApply(HttpResponse::body)
    .thenAccept(System.out::println)
    .exceptionally(ex -> {
        logger.error("request failed", ex);
        return null;
    });
```

**Fan-out, fan-in**:
```java
List<URI> urls = List.of(url1, url2, url3);

List<CompletableFuture<String>> futures = urls.stream()
    .map(u -> HttpRequest.newBuilder(u).GET().build())
    .map(r -> client.sendAsync(r, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()))
    .map(f -> f.thenApply(HttpResponse::body))
    .toList();

CompletableFuture<List<String>> all = CompletableFuture
    .allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0]))
    .thenApply(v -> futures.stream().map(CompletableFuture::join).toList());

List<String> bodies = all.join();
```

**Chain with retry**:
```java
CompletableFuture<HttpResponse<String>> withRetry(HttpRequest req, int attempts) {
    CompletableFuture<HttpResponse<String>> cf = client.sendAsync(req, BodyHandlers.ofString());
    for (int i = 1; i < attempts; i++) {
        cf = cf.thenCompose(r ->
            r.statusCode() >= 500
                ? client.sendAsync(req, BodyHandlers.ofString())
                : CompletableFuture.completedFuture(r));
    }
    return cf;
}
```

**Backpressure / streaming**:
```java
client.sendAsync(req, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofLines())
    .thenAccept(resp -> resp.body()
        .filter(line -> !line.isBlank())
        .forEach(this::processLine));
```

**HTTP/2 is negotiated via ALPN** on TLS connections automatically:
```java
HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_2)  // prefer HTTP/2, fall back to HTTP/1.1
    .build();

// Response tells you which version was actually used
HttpResponse<String> resp = client.send(req, BodyHandlers.ofString());
resp.version();  // HTTP_2 or HTTP_1_1
```

**HTTP/2 multiplexing benefits**:
- Multiple concurrent requests share a single TCP connection.
- No head-of-line blocking at the HTTP layer (still at TCP level).
- Header compression (HPACK).
- Server push (theoretically; rarely used).
- The client automatically manages streams; you don't see stream IDs.

**WebSocket**:
```java
CompletableFuture<WebSocket> wsFuture = client.newWebSocketBuilder()
    .buildAsync(URI.create("wss://echo.websocket.org"),
        new WebSocket.Listener() {
            @Override
            public CompletionStage<?> onText(WebSocket ws, CharSequence data, boolean last) {
                System.out.println("received: " + data);
                ws.request(1);
                return null;
            }
            @Override
            public void onOpen(WebSocket ws) {
                ws.sendText("hello", true);
                ws.request(1);
            }
        });

WebSocket ws = wsFuture.join();
ws.sendText("another message", true);
ws.sendClose(WebSocket.NORMAL_CLOSURE, "bye");
```

**Authentication**:
```java
HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .authenticator(new Authenticator() {
        @Override
        protected PasswordAuthentication getPasswordAuthentication() {
            return new PasswordAuthentication("user", "password".toCharArray());
        }
    })
    .build();
```

**Proxy and SSL**:
```java
HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .proxy(ProxySelector.of(new InetSocketAddress("proxy.corp", 8080)))
    .sslContext(customSSLContext())
    .sslParameters(customSSLParams())
    .build();
```

**Custom `Executor` for async**:
```java
Executor executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor();  // Java 21
HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .executor(executor)
    .build();
```

Before Java 21, the pattern for scaling HTTP calls was:
- Use the **async API** (`sendAsync`) for parallelism.
- Accept the complexity of `CompletableFuture` chains.
- Tune thread pools carefully.

With Java 21 virtual threads, the **synchronous API** scales to massive concurrency:
```java
try (var exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    List<Future<String>> futures = urls.stream()
        .map(u -> exec.submit(() -> {
            HttpRequest req = HttpRequest.newBuilder(u).GET().build();
            return client.send(req, BodyHandlers.ofString()).body();
            // synchronous blocking call — no CompletableFuture needed!
        }))
        .toList();

    List<String> bodies = futures.stream()
        .map(f -> {
            try { return f.get(); }
            catch (Exception e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
        })
        .toList();
}
```

**Why this is better**:
- Sequential-style code, easy to read and debug.
- Virtual threads unmount from carrier threads when blocked on I/O — no thread pool saturation.
- Scales to **millions of concurrent requests**.
- Stack traces point directly at your code.
- No need to chain `thenApply` / `thenCompose`.

**HttpClient works with virtual threads out of the box** — no pinning issues because the client uses NIO internally, not `synchronized` I/O.

**When to still use `sendAsync`**:
- Composing with other `CompletableFuture`-based APIs.
- You need non-thread-based concurrency (reactive).
- You're on Java 17 LTS (virtual threads require 21+).

On Java 21 for pure HTTP workloads, prefer the **sync API + virtual threads**. It's the simplest mental model and performance matches async.
